<h2 id="h-safe-draw-weight-progression">Safe Draw-Weight Progression on the Legit Maxx</h2>
<p>The 10&ndash;70 lb range is the Legit Maxx&rsquo;s biggest selling point, and also the easiest thing to misuse. Too much weight too soon is the fastest way to build bad form: hunched shoulders, sky-drawing, punching the release and, in younger shooters, real risk of shoulder strain.</p>
<?= callout('warn', 'Start lower than you think', 'A good rule: the archer should be able to draw the bow smoothly while seated, with the arrow pointed at the target, and hold at full draw for 20&ndash;30 seconds without shaking. If they have to lift the bow skyward or lean back to draw it, the weight is too high. Drop it.') ?>
<p>Here is a sample progression that Better Outdoors suggests as a starting framework. These are general guidelines, not rules from Bear; individual strength varies, and a coach or pro shop should make the final call.</p>
<?= data_table(
    ['Stage', 'Typical draw weight', 'Focus', 'When to move up'],
    [
        ['First lessons', '10–20 lb', 'Stance, anchor, follow-through, safety', 'Draws smoothly and holds 30 seconds with no strain'],
