        ['Advanced youth / adult beginner', '35–50 lb', 'Longer distances, shooting from a seated position', 'Still smooth when tired, cold or at an angle'],
        ['Hunting weight', '40 lb and up (check your state minimum)', 'Broadhead accuracy and arrow energy', 'Only after meeting legal and ethical energy needs'],
    ],
    'Sample Legit Maxx draw-weight progression'
) ?>
<p>Increase weight in small steps, a few pounds at a time, and give the archer a few weeks at each setting. Every time you change draw weight, check that the arrows are still correctly spined. Many states set minimum draw weights or energy requirements for big game, so look up your local regulations before the first hunt. Our kinetic energy guidance and calculators can help confirm a youth setup is ready.</p>

<h2 id="h-adjusting-draw-length-as-they-grow">Adjusting Bear Legit Maxx Draw Length as an Archer Grows</h2>
<p>Draw length is the other half of the fit. Retailer copy for the Legit family says adjustments are made with an Allen wrench and do not require a bow press. That is convenient, but it is not the same as &ldquo;set it and forget it.&rdquo;</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Measure first.</strong> Use the wingspan method (arm span in inches divided by 2.5) as a starting point, then confirm at full draw.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-check every few months</strong> for growing kids, and at the start of each season.</li>
  <li><strong>Move the peep</strong> after any draw length change so it lines up naturally at anchor.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-check arrow length and spine.</strong> A longer draw may need longer, stiffer arrows.</li>
  <li><strong>Re-sight</strong> the pins after any change.</li>
</ol>
<p>Get a quick starting number for any archer with our calculator:</p>
